  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nAn upper-level shortwave trough along with abundant moisture from \nthe remnants of Tropical Storm Vance will bring rainfall for much of \nthe region Thursday through Friday evening. Scattered to numerous \nshowers/storms will begin across the Big Bend and Lower Trans Pecos \nThursday morning before spreading northward by the afternoon and \nevening. Precipitable Water values (PWATs) exceed the 90th \nclimatological percentile for areas across the Lower Trans Pecos, \nBig Bend, and Permian Basin. These values indicate the threat of \nheavy rainfall leading to flash flooding. Guidance looks to have a \nbrief window of a storm or two becoming strong Thursday afternoon, \nwhere gusty winds (up to 55 mph) may occur. Overall, the severe \nweather threat remains very low and flash flooding due to multiple \nrounds of heavy rainfall will be the primary threat. Medium to long-\nrange guidance has the heavy rainfall threat peaking Thursday night \nthrough early Friday afternoon as rain coverage becomes numerous to \nwidespread during this timeframe. Urban and low-lying areas will be \nthe most susceptible spots for flash flooding. Uncertainty still \nremains where exactly the heaviest rainfall will happen and precise \nrainfall amounts. Ensembles show medium probabilities (40-70%) of \ntwo inches or more of rainfall across the Lower Trans Pecos \nextending into the northern Permian Basin. Isolated amounts up to 6\" \nmay occur per longer range high resolution models. By Friday \nevening, the upper-level shortwave trough lifts northeast. As a \nresult, lingering light showers hang around Friday night across the \nfar eastern half of the area, though little impacts (flash flooding) \nare expected by that time. \n\nSubtle upper ridging builds back into the region Saturday afternoon \nthrough early Sunday bringing drier and warmer weather. Highs are \nexpected to range from the 70s to low 80s both days. Another upper-\nlevel system approaches from the west, supplying additional low (10-\n40%) rain chances late Sunday afternoon through Monday morning. This \nsystem will eventually send a cold front by early next week. \nGuidance does have an upper-low deepening near the region which will \nbe favorable for strong winds. This type of setup can be favorable \nfor potential gap winds at Guadalupe Pass, along with blowing dust \nconcerns mainly across open fields and construction sites. Much \ncooler temperatures are forecast by early to mid next week as \nsurface high pressure builds in from the Rockies ushering in colder \nair to the region. \n\nLamberson",
